.Dd August 31, 2026
.Dt CB-REVIEW 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cb-review
.Nd cb's isolated review model
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr cb 1 Ns 's
.Cm review ,
.Cm review-local ,
.Cm refresh ,
and
.Cm review-shell
commands let you look at someone else's branch, a GitHub pull request,
or a live directory of generated output, incrementally and without
ever touching your own checkout's
.Pa .git .
The model is adapted from
.Lk https://github.com/ryaninvents/rapid-review rapid-review .
.Sh THE ISOLATED REVIEW REPO
.Ic cb review Ar key branch
creates a real, linked
.Xr git-worktree 1
of the project repo, checked out at the full content of
.Ar branch .
Because it is a genuine worktree, plain
.Xr git 1
and
.Xr gh 1
work in it exactly as anywhere else \(em
.Ic git log ,
.Ic git status ,
.Ic git remote ,
.Ic gh pr view --web
\(em with no special setup.
Alongside it,
.Nm cb
creates a second, separate git directory \(em the
.Em review repo
\(em that shares the project's object database via
.Pa objects/info/alternates
but keeps its own
.Ev HEAD ,
index, and refs, entirely independent of the worktree's own
.Ev HEAD
and index.
The two are wired together for the reviewer via
.Ev GIT_DIR
.Pq the review repo
and
.Ev GIT_WORK_TREE
.Pq the same directory, as a working tree
whenever
.Nm cb
spawns a shell for it, so
.Xr git 1
commands run there see the review repo's view instead:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev HEAD Pq the reviewed baseline
Starts at
.Ic git merge-base Ar branch Ar base ,
where
.Ar base
is
.Fl Fl base
if given, else the project's default branch.
The commit it started at is recorded as
.Ic refs/cb/base .
.Fl Fl no-merge-base
starts it at
.Ar base
directly instead \(em compare against the tip rather than the point of
divergence.
.It the working tree
Always holds the
.Em full
content of
.Ar branch ,
not a diff.
.It Ic git status
Is therefore exactly
.Ev HEAD
.Pq the reviewed baseline
compared against the full target content: exactly what is left to
review.
.El
.Pp
You review incrementally from inside a review shell
.Pq Cm review Fl Fl shell No or Cm review-shell :
.Ic git add
a file to mark it reviewed,
.Ic git commit
to checkpoint a batch and advance
.Ev HEAD .
.Ic cb refresh
fetches new upstream commits and updates the working tree
.Pq via a plain Ic git reset --hard No in the worktree's own frame ,
so amended code you already reviewed and committed resurfaces as
changed again, without discarding your committed review progress or
requiring a rebase.
This also keeps the worktree's own
.Ev HEAD
and history current, so a plain
.Ic cd
into it after refreshing still shows
.Ar branch Ns 's
latest commits.
.Pp
It also re-derives
.Ar base
the same way it was first computed and, if it moved, 3-way-merges the
delta into
.Ev HEAD :
.Bl -bullet
.It
Cleanly-merged paths advance
.Ev HEAD
to match
.Ar base Ns 's
new content, so a base-branch change you never touched \(em including one
the author merged or rebased into
.Ar branch
\(em reads as already-reviewed rather than muddying the diff with someone
else's already-landed code.
.It
A path where
.Ar base Ns 's
change conflicts with your already-reviewed content keeps the reviewed
version in
.Ev HEAD .
Since the working tree does carry
.Ar base Ns 's
change (once
.Ar branch
has absorbed it),
.Ic git status
flags that path as pending again instead of silently accepting either
side.
.El
.Pp
.Fl Fl no-advance-base
skips all of this and only updates the working tree, exactly as
.Nm cb
did before 0.6.0.
.Sh REVIEWING A PULL REQUEST
.Ic cb review Ar key Ar N
or
.Ic cb review Ar key Ar https://github.com/o/r/pull/N
resolves
.Ar N
via
.Ic gh pr view
.Pq requires Xr gh 1 , authenticated
to its head branch, then reviews that branch exactly as above.
The worktree key becomes
.Ic pr- Ns Ar N ;
the PR's title and author are recorded and shown by
.Ic cb ls
and
.Ic cb whereami .
No
.Fl Fl repo Ns / Ns Fl Fl hostname
override is passed to
.Xr gh 1 ,
so this resolves against whichever host the project's git remote
points at, GitHub Enterprise included, as long as
.Ic gh auth login --hostname Ar host
has been run once for that host.
.Pp
A PR from a fork has no
.Ic origin/ Ns Ar branch
to compare against locally, so
.Nm cb
refuses with a pointer to fetching
.Ic refs/pull/ Ns Ar N Ns /head
manually and reviewing that local branch by name instead.
.Sh REVIEWING A LIVE DIRECTORY
.Ic cb review-local Ar key dir
compares a live directory \(em not a git worktree, e.g. AI-generated
output living anywhere on disk \(em against the project's base branch,
using the same
.Ev HEAD Ns -as-baseline mechanism.
Since there is no target branch to fetch, the directory itself is never
touched by
.Ic cb refresh
\(em it tracks the directory live.
What
.Ic cb refresh
.Em does
do is re-fetch
.Ar base
and, if it moved, advance
.Ev HEAD
the same way a remote review's does
.Pq Sx THE ISOLATED REVIEW REPO ,
so a long-running local review doesn't drift as
.Ar base
lands commits.
A file
.Ar base
newly added will show as deleted in
.Ic git status
if the live directory doesn't have it \(em an honest reflection of what's
actually there, not a bug.
Unlike a remote review,
.Ic cb done
.Pq Cm review-done
never deletes
.Ar dir
itself \(em only the review repo \(em since it is a directory you own,
not one
.Nm cb
created.
.Sh INSIDE A REVIEW SHELL
.Ic cb review Fl Fl shell
or
.Ic cb review-shell
opens
.Ev SHELL
in the worktree with
.Ev GIT_DIR ,
.Ev GIT_WORK_TREE ,
and
.Ev CB_REVIEW
.Pq "project-key worktree-key"
set.
The
.Ic cb
shell function recognizes three bare verbs there, layered on top of
its normal dispatch:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic cb refresh
Equivalent to
.Ic cb refresh Ar key worktree-key ,
inferred from
.Ev CB_REVIEW .
.It Ic cb exit
Prompts for confirmation, then leaves the shell.
Nothing is deleted.
.It Ic cb done
Prompts for confirmation, then leaves the shell and deletes the
worktree
.Pq unless it is a Cm review-local review
and the review repo.
.El
